[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188045 :question/id #uuid "acb4923c-ce04-5cd5-af01-2f078a18ceed" :question/math-academy-id "e-10342" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x), & 1\\le x\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x), & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\textrm{Var}\\big[Y \\,\\big|\\, X=1 \\big]$ given that the corresponding expected value is $\\mu_{Y|1}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[Y^2 \\,| \\, X=x \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\textrm E\\big[ Y \\,|\\, X = x \\big]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $1 \\leq x \\leq e, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ Outside this domain, we have $f_{Y|X} (y \\,|\\, x) = 0.$\n\nNotice that $f_{Y|X} (y \\,|\\, x)$ does not depend on $x.$ Therefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{3}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=1]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188046 :question/id #uuid "3764cb54-7fd2-584a-9dc7-eda88368b032" :question/math-academy-id "e-10352" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\textrm{Var}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=1\\big]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $\\mu_{Y|1} = \\dfrac{9}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)  = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n| $f_{Y | X}(y \\,|\\, x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{(\\frac{9}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+1+\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{81}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188072 :question/id #uuid "3a22ccc6-495b-5b89-8b7d-775e6e620fe0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10454" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/4a/4a0e63105483b697842963df430c5341abef68b83501e9d63d17653b88309c59.svg)\n\nIf $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12},$ $\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac{5}{8},$ and $\\textrm{E}[XY]=\\dfrac{17}{48},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/e7/e7e614e58780e1600532e0cd172674c921feaae0ff35b47a01d0befaa66230ac.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\textrm{E}[Y^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x{y}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{10}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}-{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{960}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{48}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n![](images/a1/a16a9b39d5ff75935e7394d1ff0f3eb03437bc000038b20925d1f91985386b48.svg)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188205 :question/id #uuid "366bcb1d-3fe4-5047-8306-bd28935b5431" :question/math-academy-id "e-631" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -5\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 5\\cos\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{ 5\\cos\\theta }{  -5\\sin\\theta} =-\\cot\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188210 :question/id #uuid "1e1f0ecb-6f45-53ac-ae5d-ff953567aefa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9188" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d55ab8270e18d87100db9634a044d47e9314420674e8045a20cecd9b3da5542e.svg)\n\nFind $P(X \\geq 3, Y \\leq  2).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{6{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y) \\in \\left[2,5\\right]\\times \\left[1,3\\right].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n![](images/eb/eb900092e22a38e1bbc0b26853647353d5d21fc0f46c56842525e9b11d5392a1.svg)\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{5y}{6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}(\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4x}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91c41cc-51b6-5e81-94b5-47e53eefedb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185509} {:answer/id #uuid "de2b3a92-bca2-5955-9f57-985d493d01c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185510} {:answer/id #uuid "1149e63f-1791-5d3e-9b6e-48e6553bdacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185511} {:answer/id #uuid "364e336f-8c62-54e4-a088-dac539c2c563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185512} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3f60e0-b192-58fc-95d2-6460f2bd7ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1149e63f-1791-5d3e-9b6e-48e6553bdacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185511} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b94b62-4b4a-5bbe-b08a-36b39f4d7d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24fd9d96-138b-563d-a252-06faa557f32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-110420" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $\\text{E}[4X+6Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4X+6Y] & =4\\,\\text{E}[X]+6\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{3}{4})+6(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "846c0b84-2cc4-5eb4-8e68-3058bdf9bd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0 < p < \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185551} {:answer/id #uuid "d212c253-138a-5222-b90e-79de150a23ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 < p < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185552} {:answer/id #uuid "f00cc8dc-c7ff-5295-8879-d649c8167d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185553} {:answer/id #uuid "2da927b3-270a-502d-b40a-4bd7ebab1469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3} < p < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185554} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6c2780-74d5-5aea-b64e-e21caac5ea75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3} < p < \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185555}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5431da94-8cfa-5590-bcb8-49a6dad44390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185550}] :question/id #uuid "6ab4929e-9eea-5243-be49-58cd36a247bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125945" :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(\\frac{3p}{4})^{n}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{6 - 2p})$ converge?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1fcacac-6f53-5420-bcc5-b4c8bc414d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185671} {:answer/id #uuid "e31a19f6-7170-5218-8a58-2e1fe11c1788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185672} {:answer/id #uuid "191a406d-2137-5f80-a01e-b302c15ebe9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185673} {:answer/id #uuid "62a93a91-d75e-59ff-b921-802b7b3c4ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185674} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0fcff4-324b-568a-b9bc-6c33d6ad2210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185675}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb8fa77-21e1-58a7-966d-1bfeb2876f5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185670}] :question/id #uuid "479ec470-168d-5c52-9afd-ac83faf58106" :question/math-academy-id "q-143532" :question/problem "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{10}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $μ$ and population variance $σ^{2}$. If $μ$ is known and $σ$ is unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{10}∑_(i = 1)^(10)X_{i}$\n2. $∑_(i = 1)^(10)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}$\n3. $∑_(i = 1)^(10)(\\frac{X_{i} - μ}{σ})^{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1a30c16-211c-5885-bd73-60eba0b26337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185677} {:answer/id #uuid "eebe674e-a895-53a5-8284-bb2fe739487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185678} {:answer/id #uuid "00554a1e-d43f-5aca-89a9-07841ea6af07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185679} {:answer/id #uuid "3472b7ea-9bc6-58cd-bf1a-0790d8d48e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185680} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8d629-42cc-5562-a4b8-39f1b9893e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00554a1e-d43f-5aca-89a9-07841ea6af07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185679} :answer-field/id #uuid "320ef1c8-e7ec-58a4-b0b7-68dce67c7b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46af20ff-a82f-5003-946a-59bcece07f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143555" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi /2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot (-1)}{0+2\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b42bf3a8c1b5f8755e02a162e1554904bbc432285cb8ba8c7b06181e1cc03.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6cd7e58-adc6-5d97-83dc-0ef80f38597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185683} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe0e570-a22a-5641-93a8-f6671eba3fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185684} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2627b4-92aa-5fc7-b855-d9ad614c65ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185685} {:answer/id #uuid "37a2400b-425f-50cd-9cf2-4636a9355424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185686} {:answer/id #uuid "61adcb0d-059f-5544-a8ef-924174a4c953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe0e570-a22a-5641-93a8-f6671eba3fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185684} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6707d96-ec88-5b35-acfc-f90c52c72973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9cda14ea-b780-59bc-8a48-d618ff7d920f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143587" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b4cbcb39ad80d4401adffd450aed9e222663c469f1cf1cf36cbd900b5eb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68ad5e9-7fe7-596a-8c2b-19ee3b81de60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185785} {:answer/id #uuid "15178949-e15f-5295-b833-173787b1d3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} {:answer/id #uuid "23daea13-16e4-5115-a876-009e017926b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185787} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24e81a-7cd0-52ae-a4ec-7a2d4936cd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185788} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecf83c7-e917-54b8-9b79-79ccd5e0603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15178949-e15f-5295-b833-173787b1d3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb7bcb5-fc17-5e6e-81c2-7dacca31243a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c577835a-f79d-5fd0-ba9c-ce150ab9e53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144828" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d3726847858c1afc66497820a48fa6d6bbe39e4d909b84b562a2daeea085bb4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function comprises $2$ triangles, $2$ rectangles, and $1$ trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/99/99f558b3c1d86c5638543361a63823f13a9e01f2cc5042d0f781080f9f33c8a2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{3}{2}+1) \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+2+4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}\\}=\\frac{19}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{19}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2452ce83-dc52-55f6-8ada-4270840e5f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186103} {:answer/id #uuid "3112bbd0-2efd-5af4-9183-d529b43787f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186104} {:answer/id #uuid "32a79aa0-324a-576c-b24a-9dda030a3055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186105} {:answer/id #uuid "496b21b0-10f4-5ed9-a567-35580e353507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186106} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5cc2f6-63db-5ee3-a19a-a655c587bd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496b21b0-10f4-5ed9-a567-35580e353507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186106} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0e8466-e71c-51fa-a1f3-58418768c396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87429b89-e333-5492-85d9-bf5d6ddc4038" :question/math-academy-id "q-145581" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|0}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =[(0)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}]-(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aac95833-5753-59ed-9064-3dce9eb6bf55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186247} {:answer/id #uuid "10e80e17-c1da-540f-920f-b561444e71ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186248} {:answer/id #uuid "391b2f77-21a7-5d46-a4db-4f999030277f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186249} {:answer/id #uuid "72246038-c9c6-578f-bb17-dada1cfec565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186250} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9b3625-cb76-501b-acae-fb328a041f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72246038-c9c6-578f-bb17-dada1cfec565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186250} :answer-field/id #uuid "a645beee-fbf3-56f4-b659-c712fa82e7d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc5459e2-e87e-5998-9738-d0d2044cb502" :question/math-academy-id "q-145870" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}]$ given that the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{7}{12}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{\\frac{1}{2}+y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+y)}{1+2y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})}{1+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{2x+1}{2}=x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{6})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}]-{\\mu }_{X|\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{49}{144} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4abe7f7-40a7-5fee-8c26-2ce6a49da4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186403} {:answer/id #uuid "02c37c21-5706-5110-839d-3b7f23f1c8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186404} {:answer/id #uuid "28522e3b-eba5-534c-bcd9-5ef42a50ee9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186405} {:answer/id #uuid "42159274-25fc-5902-8f54-b1273ec708fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186406} {:answer/id #uuid "56837666-e2e8-5a77-9c5c-5bd3b0c1b2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4abe7f7-40a7-5fee-8c26-2ce6a49da4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186403} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b741eef-9c5a-588c-9901-de8a736211a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6e8480d-863e-574b-98a7-52fe067fa786" :question/math-academy-id "q-146230" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|1}=\\frac{6}{5}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{Y|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}]-{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{36}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984380c5-40cc-5710-a30c-a6c66b2ff180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186415} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b9fba5-d63e-5377-857e-f317e4e33b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{103}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186416} {:answer/id #uuid "d576a0de-cee9-5266-b5b8-30152891dc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{110}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186417} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb7bf53-7c58-55f8-abd8-0cd422ffc050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{70}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186418} {:answer/id #uuid "7044a4a3-0a43-582f-817a-e99488b07711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d576a0de-cee9-5266-b5b8-30152891dc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{110}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186417} :answer-field/id #uuid "f443ee10-4ae5-53b4-b5be-0af99b1c94bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07c19340-2d1a-5056-9657-cee2e32e9b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-146289" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|0}=\\frac{13}{3}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{9}{20}$ | $\\frac{11}{20}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{11}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{11}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{11}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{9}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}]-{(\\frac{13}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =0+4+27-\\frac{169}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af426a61-873a-54e8-87e7-c5268f358e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186613} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a125c4-79a7-5fc7-8d69-81df40d1a785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186614} {:answer/id #uuid "104fbeb9-321b-5cf2-a901-4e2171ef33e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186615} {:answer/id #uuid "291d2635-4024-5090-9a86-02fd630f782c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186616} {:answer/id #uuid "59ac25e1-139f-55c2-acdd-07c0ae67efdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "104fbeb9-321b-5cf2-a901-4e2171ef33e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186615} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff21e41-edd8-56fc-b9a8-ac593324b506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab2b344e-989b-53e3-aa40-0cb9cb3e7391" :question/math-academy-id "q-146773" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2{y}^{7/2}}, & \\,1\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|3}=\\frac{5}{3}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=3] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{y}^{7/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2{y}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-5\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=3]-{\\mu }_{Y|3}^{2} \\\\  & =5-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{25}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820466ba-6f36-5d47-afef-0ee20288f099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186625} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa1bcc-91b5-57e8-85ba-c02fe35e14e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186626} {:answer/id #uuid "cb641e71-4763-57b7-bdbf-54ac5c041138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186627} {:answer/id #uuid "f74163e9-f3d4-5fad-816e-1c659459e6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186628} {:answer/id #uuid "08a51dff-0f49-52c1-ab38-9208cc65b517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb641e71-4763-57b7-bdbf-54ac5c041138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186627} :answer-field/id #uuid "2338bb54-2402-5bab-b83e-832eb2b270cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cefc73e-1ad2-58be-a1de-e39c1b293d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-146806" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}, & \\,x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-2]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|-2}=\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-2] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-2}}{(-2)}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=-2] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=-2]-{\\mu }_{X|-2}^{2} \\\\  & =2-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01df44a6-657a-5670-a985-4a87b7716922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547320} {:answer/id #uuid "0a71cefc-8de5-5de6-a1e7-e827ad772d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547337} {:answer/id #uuid "2529513a-9fa9-5a66-b66c-95b167283ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547386} {:answer/id #uuid "b1914b83-6794-52fe-91db-700c3ef7d55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547703} {:answer/id #uuid "ef02e2b8-d181-5dc2-952d-5f459a779602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a71cefc-8de5-5de6-a1e7-e827ad772d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547337} :answer-field/id #uuid "f13c53ef-1e0a-55f0-86d4-e8d2d1425533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "150d719c-c9e6-5c71-9c3c-1c207b1e3bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148189" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ and $E[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{5}{12},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{144} & -\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{144} & \\frac{11}{144}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d5b1e0c8-d87e-5f56-8be3-b332673bb90b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187530} {:answer-field/id #uuid "7fe96279-8417-5715-a45f-5b4fafe3ebff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187531}] :question/id #uuid "16cb25c2-cafa-54b0-a048-204836724506" :question/math-academy-id "q-251351" :question/problem "Let $X_{i}∼Geom(\\frac{1}{3})$ for $i = 1, 2, …, 60$ be independent geometric random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = ∑_(i = 1)^(60)X_{i}$.\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?The approximate distribution is $X∼N($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "902e918d-f280-5f87-b606-c00cf9342bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p < - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187645} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f38c25-8e19-573b-aa57-85349e1e1e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4 < p < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187646} {:answer/id #uuid "e67c347a-c238-5557-ab59-ffae20bd9622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 < p < 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187647} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f1426-c38b-5862-91ca-53f3575c4d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 < p < 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187648} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8d897c-6733-54e5-a5cb-1aa6ea2ad083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p > 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187649}] :answer-field/id #uuid "11d5de50-6c2c-5e71-a0b0-d640218aebb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187644}] :question/id #uuid "7c27494e-818f-53cd-9356-5c9bf0b4e588" :question/math-academy-id "q-50534" :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(\\frac{p}{4})^{n}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(\\frac{4}{n})^{p}$ converge?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a7fc26-e011-5f8a-9014-80acfc559327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p > 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187657} {:answer/id #uuid "a957ce20-7908-529a-b10b-6287510c7d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2} < p < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187658} {:answer/id #uuid "aaacf0b7-05b5-504c-a16f-ebb3a0f35cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 < p < \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187659} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bf53bc-f27f-542f-b526-6980aa56ddf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2} < p < 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187660} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0a7046-d377-56dc-b606-2dc8f00ac998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p < - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187661}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d890c7a7-2a96-577a-94db-6e68e318385f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187656}] :question/id #uuid "ae866df5-96e8-5d13-88f2-b8d70b97d92c" :question/math-academy-id "q-50954" :question/problem "For what values of $p$ will both $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)5(\\frac{p}{3})^{n}$ and $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2p}}$ converge?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192391 :question/id #uuid "19e7744e-9d87-5ad1-9648-029e7dc8e565" :question/math-academy-id "e-10113" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1^2),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8, \\qquad \n\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2.\n$$\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta$ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8,$ $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2,$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{8}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(25.2-2\\theta \\cdot 11.8+8{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-12.6+11.8\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192416 :question/id #uuid "db6692a1-b033-5507-aead-ea2837768687" :question/math-academy-id "e-10516" :question/problem "Find an approximation for the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point between the curves\n$f(x)= 5 - \\sqrt x, \\qquad g(x)= \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{x}{5}\\right)$\nthat lies to the right of the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-values of the intersection points, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)-g(x) = 0\n$$\n\nAs a result, the problem can be reduced to finding the roots of the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(x) &= f(x) - g(x) \\\\\n&= (5 - \\sqrt{x}) - \\sin (\\frac{x}{5}) \\\\\n&= 5 - \\sqrt{x} - \\sin (\\frac{x}{5})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we plot $y=h(x)$ using a graphing calculator to find an interval that contains the root. Since $h(x)$ contains trigonometric functions, you need to make sure your calculator is in $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{RAD}\\,}$ (radians) mode.\n\nTo plot the function, we press\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,y=\\,}\n$$\n\nand enter the function definition in the first available space. We then press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{graph}\\,}$ to graph the function.\n\nThe default view on most graphing calculators is $x \\in [-10,10], y \\in [-10,10]$. However, in this instance, the root falls outside the default view.\n\nTo get a better view, we might need to either\n\n- zoom in or out, or\n- adjust the window ranges.\n\nWe can adjust the window settings using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{window}\\,}$ button (or equivalent).\n\nIn our case, the appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [15,35]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-2,2]$\n\nThis gives us the following graph:\n\n![](images/2a/2a152b2535a9878caa01785d593094e104b26c7c7d694c67e0c549e047eae768.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the root lies inside the interval $x\\in [25,30]$.\n\nTo get a good approximation for the root, we use the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{zero}\\,}$ command (or equivalent). This is usually accessed by pressing the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$ button (or equivalent). Note that we sometimes need to press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{2nd}\\,}$ or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$ button first.\n\nWe enter the following when prompted:\n\n- The \"left bound\" should be a value close to the root on its left-hand side. Let's pick $25$.\n- The \"right bound\" should be a value close to the root on its right-hand side. Let's pick $30$.\n- The \"guess\" could be any number from $[25,30]$. Let's pick $28$.\n\n![](images/01/011c3bf09d7984a8bd03a1f1c535b7d7bd11a088f06a5c83f89599d2070d0cd1.png)\n\nThe calculator returns the zero\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nx \\approx 29.291\\,697\n$$\n\nRounding to $3$ decimal places, we obtain the following:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nx \\approx 29.292\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192427 :question/id #uuid "20ceb54a-f4aa-57ea-9a1a-142a7baa1495" :question/math-academy-id "e-10551" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192428 :question/id #uuid "78d2ffbd-3159-5222-ae23-41411388a94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10552" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/9a/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192434 :question/id #uuid "415295e3-8f7a-581f-8433-33b10c3faba4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10593" :question/problem "![](images/54/54f117c57d1c3bb1db17085a74ac83bb8304ddd8c84bb5f213c0c4456b1f5a53.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle{AOB}$ is $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ of a full turn. What is the degree measure of the angle?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{60} \\cdot 360^{∘} &= \\frac{1 \\cdot 360^{∘}}{60} \\\\ &= \\frac{360^{∘}}{60} \\\\ &= (\\frac{360}{60})^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this division problem, we first divide the numerator and denominator by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{360}{60}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{360 \\div  10}{60 \\div  10}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{36 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0}}{6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{36}{6}} & \\displaystyle \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the fraction by expressing it as a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{6} &= 36 \\div 6 \\\\ &= 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ is $6^\\circ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192435 :question/id #uuid "29e61984-9a23-54bd-b7dc-175f3958977d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10601" :question/problem "![](images/37/37e229d14775daf5cb2958010ba8be408bd84256661457ea0dd255195aaf1960.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle{AOB}$ is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of a full turn. Expressed as an improper fraction, what is the degree measure of the angle?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 360^{∘} &= \\frac{1 \\cdot 360^{∘}}{12} \\\\ &= \\frac{360^{∘}}{12} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's common to write the fraction in parentheses and place the degree symbol outside, as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{360}{12})^{∘}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192452 :question/id #uuid "23ed7849-0c04-59a3-a648-3a451052fbaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10665" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\vert 2x-3 \\vert = -x+1$." :question/worked-solution "We have the following two cases:\n\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=2x-3$, we obtain\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; 2x - 3|=-x + 1; 2x|=-x + 4; 3x|= 4; x|= \\frac{4}{3}$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; \\mid 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} - 3 \\mid |= - (\\frac{4}{3}) + 1; \\mid \\frac{8}{3} - 3 \\mid |=-\\frac{4}{3} + 1; \\mid \\frac{8}{3} - \\frac{9}{3} \\mid |=-\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{3}{3}; \\mid - \\frac{1}{3} \\mid |=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{1}{3}|=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot$\nSince $x=-\\dfrac 43$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=-(2x-3)$, we obtain\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; - (2x - 3)|=-x + 1; - 2x + 3|=-x + 1; 3|= x + 1; 2|= x; x|= 2$.\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 2x - 3 \\mid |=-x + 1; \\mid 2(2) - 3 \\mid |= - (2) + 1; \\mid 4 - 3 \\mid |=-2 + 1; \\mid 1 \\mid |=-1; 1|=-1\\cdot$\nSince $x=2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192455 :question/id #uuid "d07984c0-c35f-5b86-83e4-51f9fa15512f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10687" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192483 :question/id #uuid "072dbfa6-e4bd-57fe-b855-676cd16d9032" :question/math-academy-id "e-10896" :question/problem "![](images/82/82da00165dde65dd7fcc20ab90e3b7577f66e4e0eae61992fc0dbc7b93de4dfd.png)\n\nFunctions $f$ and $g$ are represented by the graphs above. If $h(x)=g(f(x))$, what is $h'\\left(2\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = g'(f(x)) \\cdot f'(x)\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nh'(2) = g'(f(2)) \\cdot f'(2)\n$$\n\nFrom the graph we obtain\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $[1,3]$ equals $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$. So, $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $\\left[1,3\\right]$ equals $-1$. So, $g'\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)=-1$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(2) &= g^{′}(f(2)) \\cdot f^{′}(2) \\\\\n&= g^{′}(\\frac{5}{2}) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192485 :question/id #uuid "399c1168-d80e-5969-9c93-0ab34f7576a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-10912" :question/problem "Consider the following ellipse: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2),$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2},$ $x=1,$ and $y=\\sqrt2,$ and solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$  We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1)+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192488 :question/id #uuid "064cfb7e-bcba-5965-ba98-43040b167ff3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10920" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd0b1175d74053c5323d1314c9a7dee30d4412cd2212e7e5bd61d61d867642b4.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. If $h(x)=e^{{f(x)}}$, what is $h'\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{f(x)}) \\\\\n&= e^{f(x)} \\cdot f^{′}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nh'\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right) = e^{f\\left(-1/2\\right)}\\cdot f'\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- From the graph, we obtain $f\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right) = 3$.\n- Additionally, the slope of $f(x)$ on $\\left[-2.5,0\\right]$ equals $1$, so $f'\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)=1$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) &= e^{f(-1/2)} \\cdot f^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= e^{3} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= e^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192498 :question/id #uuid "18eca494-51ed-5f9c-95cc-6c9dc8efd474" :question/math-academy-id "e-10963" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1\n$$\n\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ and $x=3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3})-(-2)\\cdot y & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.   \n\nAt the point where $x=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192509 :question/id #uuid "0fd784a0-5146-5f7b-9936-8d3bc43d7062" :question/math-academy-id "e-11028" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =y\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =y\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\  & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =xy(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192518 :question/id #uuid "6f7bdbcf-fb8e-5c70-b368-4e73cc94bdb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11112" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{4x-y}{x+y^2},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4x-y}{x+{y}^{2}}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2})}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot 1}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+4{y}^{2}-4x+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-1)} & =\\frac{4(-1)^{2}+(-1)}{{(2+{(-1)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192544 :question/id #uuid "232b1ad9-6651-582b-b942-b36c7896806e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12971" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2-5y}{3} \\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-5y}{3} & \\le 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2-5y}{3}) & \\le 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2-5y}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\le 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ 2-5y & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-5y & \\le 12 \\\\ 2-5y-2 & \\le 12-2 \\\\ -5y & \\le 10 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ y & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\ge -2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192545 :question/id #uuid "bab434ec-d18a-5d77-9bf1-8e16e9af2556" :question/math-academy-id "e-12974" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4y -1 > \\dfrac {5y - 2} {3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-1 & >\\frac{5y-2}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >3\\cdot (\\frac{5y-2}{3}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >\\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5y-2}{\\cancel{3}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (4y-1) & >5y-2 \\\\ 12y-3 & >5y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t12y-3\t&> 5y-2 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t12y-3-5y\t&> 5y-2-5y \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t7y-3\t&> {-2} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t7y-3 +3\t&> {-2} +3 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t7y\t\t&> {1} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{7y}{7}\t&> \\dfrac{1}7 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\ty\t\t&> \\dfrac{1}{7}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y > \\dfrac17.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192546 :question/id #uuid "8740f864-62be-5391-bf06-ac4f4e1c1a2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12975" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3a-2}{5} > \\dfrac{4+6a}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a-2}{5} & >\\frac{4+6a}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{3a-2}{5}) & >10\\cdot (\\frac{4+6a}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (3a-2) & >5\\cdot (4+6a) \\\\ 6a-4 & >20+30a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-4 & >20+30a \\\\ 6a-4-30a & >20+30a-30a \\\\ -4-24a & >20 \\\\ -4-24a+4 & >20+4 \\\\ -24a & >24 \\\\ \\frac{-24a}{-24} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{24}{-24} \\\\ a & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a < -1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192589 :question/id #uuid "3117a7dd-8934-5d82-ad99-5ac5fd1f7adc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14073" :question/problem "What is $8\\,250 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $10 \\, \\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10 \\, \\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{10mm}{cm})^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{mm}^2)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{cm}^2)$, we need to multiply $8\\,250 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{cm}^2}{100\\,\\textrm{mm}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $8\\,250\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 8 \\hspace{0.167em} 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 8 \\hspace{0.167em} 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 8 \\hspace{0.167em} 250 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 8 \\hspace{0.167em} 250 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{8 \\hspace{0.167em} 250}{100}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 82.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $8\\,250 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ is equivalent to $82.5 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192590 :question/id #uuid "bb48739a-6806-59f2-a7ca-f11195bc5788" :question/math-academy-id "e-14074" :question/problem "What is $5\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{m}^2$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{100cm}{m})^{2} &= \\frac{100^{2}cm^{2}}{m^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10000cm^{2}}{m^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{cm}^2)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{m}^2)$, we need to multiply $5\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{10\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $5\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{10 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ is equivalent to $0.5 \\,\\textrm{m}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192591 :question/id #uuid "7d729c2f-5d0d-5b2d-bec6-72f73eafe4aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14075" :question/problem "What is $470\\,000 \\,\\textrm{m}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{km}^2$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1000m}{km})^{2} &= \\frac{1000^{2}m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000000m^{2}}{km^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{m}^2)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{km}^2)$, we need to multiply $470\\,000 \\,\\textrm{m}^2$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{1\\,000\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $470\\,000 \\,\\textrm{m}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 470 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 470 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{m}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 470 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{m}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 470 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{470 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{km}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.47 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{km}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $470\\,000 \\, \\textrm{m}^2$ is equivalent to $0.47 \\,\\textrm{km}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192595 :question/id #uuid "5198a50a-1c1b-537c-aa50-887bf14dff7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15030" :question/problem "A research firm conducted a survey comparing two random samples of $10$ customers from two different gas stations in Miami. The time, in minutes per week, that customers of stations $A$ and $B$ spend at the stations is denoted by $x_i$ and $y_i$, respectively. The results are summarized below.\n$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 72, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i^2 = 548, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} y_i = 100, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} y_i^2 = 1020$\n\nGiven that the samples from each station are independent and the population variance $\\sigma^2$ in each station may be assumed to be the same, find a pooled estimate for $\\sigma^2$, rounded to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Given two independent random samples from populations with the same variance $\\sigma^2$, the pooled sample variance $S_p^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$ and is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\nS_p^2 = \\dfrac{(n_1-1)S_1^2 + (n_2-1)S_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $S_1^2$ and $S_2^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIf $n_1=n_2$, the above formula reduces to\n\n$$\nS_p^2 = \\dfrac{S_1^2 + S_2^2}{2}\n$$\n\nWe must first compute the estimates $s_1^2$ and $s_2^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{1}^{2} &= \\frac{n_{1}}{n_{1} - 1}(x^{2}^(―) - x^(―)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{10 - 1}(\\frac{548}{10} - (\\frac{72}{10})^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 3.2889 \\\\\ns_{2}^{2} &= \\frac{n_{2}}{n_{2} - 1}(y^{2}^(―) - y^(―)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{10 - 1}(\\frac{1020}{10} - (\\frac{100}{10})^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 2.2222\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the pooled variance is\n\n$$\ns_p^2 = \\dfrac{s_1^2+s_2^2}{2} \\approx\\dfrac{3.2889 + 2.222}{2} \\approx 2.8\\,\\textrm{min}^2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192596 :question/id #uuid "fa24ac5e-d56b-57a9-a9c3-5d1c94477e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-15031" :question/problem "A health technician selects random samples of newborn babies from two different hospitals. In kilograms, the babies' weights from the first and second samples are denoted as $x_i$ and $y_i$, respectively, where the sizes of each sample are $n_1 = 9$ and $n_2 = 7$. The results are summarized below.\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{9} x_i = 29.90, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{9} x_i^2 = 100.99, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{7} y_i = 24.7, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{7} y_i^2 = 89.59$\n\nGiven that the samples from each hospital are independent and the population variance $\\sigma^2$ in each hospital may be assumed to be the same, find a pooled estimate for $\\sigma^2$, rounded to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Given two independent random samples from populations with the same variance $\\sigma^2$, the pooled sample variance $S_p^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$ and is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\nS_p^2 = \\dfrac{(n_1 - 1)S_1^2 + (n_2 - 1)S_2^2}{n_1 + n_2 - 2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $S_1^2$ and $S_2^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nWe must first compute the estimates $s_1^2$ and $s_2^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{1}^{2} &= \\frac{n_{1}}{n_{1} - 1}(x^{2}^(―) - x^(―)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{9 - 1}(\\frac{100.99}{9} - (\\frac{29.90}{9})^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.2069 \\\\\ns_{2}^{2} &= \\frac{n_{2}}{n_{2} - 1}(y^{2}^(―) - y^(―)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{7 - 1}(\\frac{89.59}{7} - (\\frac{24.7}{7})^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.4057\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the pooled variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{p}^{2} &= ((9 - 1) \\cdot 0.2069 + (7 - 1) \\cdot 0.4057)/(9 + 7 - 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4.0894}{14} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.3kg^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192604 :question/id #uuid "2f1fcc43-1ad4-5997-8bf1-0001a12bec3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15063" :question/problem "Consider two paired random samples $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{27}$ and $Y_1, Y_2, \\ldots, Y_{27}$ of size $n = 27$. It is known that the differences between paired observations $D_i = X_i - Y_i$ are normally distributed with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2 = 3$. Derive the expression used to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$." :question/worked-solution "To construct a confidence interval, we start with a random variable that depends on the samples and has a known distribution under certain conditions.\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/((\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}})); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/((\\frac{1}{3}))$,\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n\nIt is known that if the samples are paired and the differences between each pair are normally distributed with a population mean $\\mu$ and known population variance, $Z$ has a standard normal distribution.\n\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n\nWe wish to find an interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce18d66a53578e13b52bc7d6b6fd44ab6320ed98795be5621ae892727e7ef8eb.png)\n\nSince we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval must be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025\n$$\n\nWe can find the endpoints of such interval using a table of values for the normal distribution.\n\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}-z_{0.025} & < Z < z_{0.025} \\\\ -z_{0.025} & < ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ)/((\\frac{1}{3})) < z_{0.025}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nto get rid of the denominator:\n\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $-\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n\nThen, we multiply all sides by $-1$:\n\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n\nNext, we add $\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}$:\n\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)$\n\nNext, we rewrite our inequality so that the smaller terms are on the left-hand side.\n\n> $x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n\nNow, we write our result more compactly.\n\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/((\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}})); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/((\\frac{1}{3}))$,\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}-z_{0.025} & < Z < z_{0.025} \\\\ -z_{0.025} & < ((x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ)/((\\frac{1}{3})) < z_{0.025}\\end{bmatrix}$\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $-\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n> $\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)$\n> $x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}$\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0534bb1f-65e3-5897-b408-1b15454e1b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235834} {:answer/id #uuid "58e7e63e-59c2-5757-97c6-f0139f93e289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236378} {:answer/id #uuid "70f3c5ad-7d3d-5f34-a7c8-40042b67b1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236536} {:answer/id #uuid "76cf070f-57b6-5eec-89de-bbd505e08d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236563} {:answer/id #uuid "d42e233f-1181-5e52-a537-6d23d2208abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0534bb1f-65e3-5897-b408-1b15454e1b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235834} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c63a5d-6485-5f47-b579-b88b04b99e65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6897be5c-1a95-5fb0-8457-e36227d8e8e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-100493" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2t)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/46/4612e37c6c611cbaa44c09f4dcc47d006ad45403fff9af5b4df7dca71693dd6e.png)\n\n \n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03dd1bfc-524a-526c-ab47-ea2176047136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564945} {:answer/id #uuid "47aa3e32-e1b5-524e-93ad-e8eb5b6262a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565042} {:answer/id #uuid "a27ca631-3d74-5d12-a5b9-dd9f1c08420e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565185} {:answer/id #uuid "b8429d24-fcb3-5137-8d24-cdf2001e2ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565214} {:answer/id #uuid "ee93ae1d-35f0-50c0-842b-0fc48ec6a8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03dd1bfc-524a-526c-ab47-ea2176047136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564945} :answer-field/id #uuid "994fb7fa-8d18-55d7-b9a1-980f563daff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3a826f8-4b21-5324-8e83-97a3d69a92ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-101941" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{7-3x}{2}>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7-3x}{2} & >2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{7-3x}{2}) & >2\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7-3x}{\\cancel{2}}) & >2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 7-3x & >4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-3x & >4 \\\\ 7-3x-7 & >4-7 \\\\ -3x & >-3 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fd4c22-89d2-5fde-b119-9ce2163bb9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549321} {:answer/id #uuid "94ffbd69-746d-5e22-8c95-44e785cd24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(x+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549607} {:answer/id #uuid "a685ea85-b22a-51d2-9d33-2ac79331c9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549630} {:answer/id #uuid "b704f50d-5c35-5b3e-a0c8-af0ac5fd5317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549658} {:answer/id #uuid "f64ff466-0bca-57e1-8856-2a4a8172d8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=9(x+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a685ea85-b22a-51d2-9d33-2ac79331c9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549630} :answer-field/id #uuid "1728263c-7515-5eab-a1d5-1f1401b6e0ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7546f9b-e164-5363-aa15-2d4ef97c5e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-148288" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}-1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+1} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{x+1})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b2900c9-1a52-5c45-aed2-605280ab5a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=27(x-4)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549548} {:answer/id #uuid "8aaf3446-c684-5564-a8b6-8388976113a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{16}{(3-x)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549581} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ed2b73-1ef1-577c-b9a7-c447148706e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64}(3-x)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549647} {:answer/id #uuid "f085bb4e-fe8a-59a1-aa2f-9be46d61a226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{27}{(4-x)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549761} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf8be41-e92b-561f-ad73-49d08a3c31d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbf8be41-e92b-561f-ad73-49d08a3c31d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549789} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e4db2ae-6667-5cf8-9f6c-1f35ce6fa735" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4c1cf18-a880-5282-8283-771dcb962b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148452" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ x-3 & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ 3-x & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3-x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{4}{3-x} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{3-x})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae6ce87e-1501-5fa6-8602-8a0b3031a3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{270}{6})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190391} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7ec882-ee24-5f78-9035-a1b38018cb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{45}{6})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190392} {:answer/id #uuid "0363a214-a369-5714-87d9-0e5ab1eca656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{180}{6})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190393} {:answer/id #uuid "08436c81-7161-5107-9db0-9be69ac1d1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{90}{6})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190394} {:answer/id #uuid "3843e4e1-a451-58a0-bd55-d4863db803cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{360}{6})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190395}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c34f64b7-5641-5b6e-a245-e579a4053046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190390}] :question/id #uuid "98aff8f9-e532-5fc1-9777-0499a253117a" :question/math-academy-id "q-150692" :question/problem "![](images/03/03762ec122eced43e97ef02a10d05baae41e608cd682cf7a36ab882a12cf4c24.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $∠AOB$ is $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a full turn. Expressed as an improper fraction, what is the degree measure of the angle?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc05fa9b-b101-5ec6-87cb-bc6941d9ed83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{230}{24})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190415} {:answer/id #uuid "d61be09c-c53a-5555-b065-bed99a246a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{180}{24})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190416} {:answer/id #uuid "fc97de73-9834-53dc-bd5f-f085022b7f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{270}{24})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190417} {:answer/id #uuid "43dc57db-4e54-5003-b721-3a18d7d81895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{90}{24})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190418} {:answer/id #uuid "4e487419-4da7-5029-8cb9-2b0f23ebb76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{360}{24})^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190419}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9377f646-8b96-534d-b858-0981558502a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190414}] :question/id #uuid "6f32efe1-8925-58fc-accd-18c7d4e3f9ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-150713" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7eb73f1e257e500909d6e42ee02aada3acc9b70d9ad9d09681d31f749e1035cf.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $∠AOB$ is $\\frac{1}{24}$ of a full turn. Expressed as an improper fraction, what is the degree measure of the angle?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4663198a-3838-5f13-81ee-0f7a862f54a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272988} {:answer/id #uuid "54b0e6f3-3d84-54cb-89bc-8fea9434af49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273109} {:answer/id #uuid "a00f1234-6c97-5b7a-8031-86c7ba170053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273769} {:answer/id #uuid "b94eae07-d565-539f-b4aa-c051afc8312b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273993} {:answer/id #uuid "d44ab79b-23d6-57b1-97be-2e3155ef226f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00f1234-6c97-5b7a-8031-86c7ba170053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a0df4a-146b-55d7-a024-0882f2023b05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7e1cf83-7aca-540b-924f-ec8936fe80cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-152667" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/c8/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eafadd22-32c9-5f02-bdd3-ab98fd712cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec (\\frac{π}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190745} {:answer/id #uuid "43b11662-168a-5e96-86ca-4e6c55e9f3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sec (\\frac{π}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190746} {:answer/id #uuid "43f0354a-e092-5ea0-9541-48fcce1513c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec (\\frac{π}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190747} {:answer/id #uuid "625ef301-b2f7-5c26-abf9-dadfab722de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sec (\\frac{π}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190748} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd0be0a-a956-5f1c-b010-0ff07424756e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec (\\frac{2π}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190749}] :answer-field/id #uuid "06ec2712-b0b7-5f16-91bf-83376153f85a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190744}] :question/id #uuid "dc07cc85-b599-533a-a316-b8b2a8fec24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156108" :question/problem "Express $\\sec (\\frac{9π}{5})$ in terms of a reference angle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138b1fcb-e9c9-5d03-b932-f16b4a7068d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin (\\frac{π}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190751} {:answer/id #uuid "2245459b-b45d-5a5b-9cfb-e17c987