th} :answer/value "n \\le N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196186}] :answer-field/id #uuid "505150da-07a5-5af4-b454-a41881b2c623" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186196184}] :question/id #uuid "503e5c47-7250-5f6b-9e5f-f0d634069b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-183389" :question/problem "The notation $lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{n^{2} + n}{2n^{2} - 1}) = \\frac{1}{2}$ means the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\n$ε > 0$,\n{{field-2}}\n$N ∈ N$,\n{{field-3}}\n$n ∈ N$,\n{{field-4}}\n$⇒ \\mid \\frac{n^{2} + n}{2n^{2} - 1} -$\n{{field-5}}\n$\\mid$\n{{field-6}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aef2d58-4f97-565f-92e2-f7a18012072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197535} {:answer/id #uuid "9859e4bd-f0ea-5c7d-9135-59281613b137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197536} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfb6463-e116-5658-af37-8050c0e1de81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197537} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdbe21a-4020-5375-bc77-32e9f4bfc13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197538} {:answer/id #uuid "567ac564-b878-51eb-8aa3-e254ea745d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aef2d58-4f97-565f-92e2-f7a18012072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197535} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42b1007-56d3-5b64-871d-19d6670e6fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ed0b17-cac6-5f50-8a04-2449a141ebc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-3442" :question/problem "![](images/72/720713e7e1dbfbc9ce3ebb486e8744327c633745d00df7dc862d794eefe57bbc.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $(4,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7(\\frac{7}{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb983ed1-12b9-5d18-ae27-ed126eb1224b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197541} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7d4a1f-44fc-53ad-840b-6602c98d35d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197542} {:answer/id #uuid "27edc3f5-62f0-5370-b88c-7dcbfa251239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197543} {:answer/id #uuid "4038e29d-700f-54a2-8a57-73f7d9349f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197544} {:answer/id #uuid "72ae41da-1f8c-51fa-a6b6-6a5084a5f4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197545}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7a419e-fda0-5f67-90f3-47f074378646" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197540} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74fcf4d5-ab6d-50ee-8a97-7f767db1d054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395866} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cd5906-d8a2-5c66-aea8-20457b721c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395867} {:answer/id #uuid "47e529a6-93ea-513d-8135-657ae263a7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395868} {:answer/id #uuid "549d228e-ff4d-5856-a054-7a41e02feaf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395869} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac4715e-6ff4-560f-92e3-b1a7dcf09470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47e529a6-93ea-513d-8135-657ae263a7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395868} :answer-field/id #uuid "badca932-e160-50c7-80e4-3918dd2ca9d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5440dc48-4c46-5737-826b-cf98fbacdffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-37701" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the product of sines of angle measures of a triangle." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0<z<\\pi ,$ we have that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\ne 0.$ On the other hand, if $y-x=\\pi \\cdot n,$ with $n$ an integer, we obtain that $x>\\pi $ or $y>\\pi ,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493498} {:answer/id #uuid "c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(1-{e}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493712} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493730} {:answer/id #uuid "f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ad75dc-fe53-5e66-9950-00b2a3fe62bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1c41fe9-56fa-5a4c-8bab-8d810d33bac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38371" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=xy$ and $v=y+x,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le y+x\\le 10\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcc626f3-af02-5a96-b9d0-30aec8f61fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375111} {:answer/id #uuid "00ed8726-d262-5be5-97e7-a1fe5ea12c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375112} {:answer/id #uuid "a7036b0a-b817-5bc3-9b4e-f5631003b120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375113} {:answer/id #uuid "92615a0a-ce00-5262-b777-63a066308685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375114} {:answer/id #uuid "db58fbc6-bb95-536c-a184-99aae57b8126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7036b0a-b817-5bc3-9b4e-f5631003b120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375113} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bce0f67-7ee4-58f3-b3ef-499ee3fa3477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6722ea8b-a918-58be-8c65-d81640229453" :question/math-academy-id "q-38377" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04edc689-9e29-5c9b-8da2-da4420287986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498740} {:answer/id #uuid "314e6482-a456-5bda-8904-887833458d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\sqrt{w}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498844} {:answer/id #uuid "57cc1828-6342-5346-b05d-7d143db10ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3\\sqrt{w}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498931} {:answer/id #uuid "9156d344-2302-5c4e-b0e7-6f7c95dca531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499047} {:answer/id #uuid "cd799ece-9c4a-56a0-b053-cc390590f85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{u+v}3w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04edc689-9e29-5c9b-8da2-da4420287986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498740} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe99af2-8c2c-533c-80a0-ca42ec737c5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96fa59d6-c144-5393-a4a7-7b8198eb987c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38941" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,1\\le x-y\\le 2,\\,1\\le z\\le (x-y)(x+y)\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\\,w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3\\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le {w}^{2}\\le uv\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3\\sqrt{z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}3w\\cdot |-w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3{w}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
